\title{Pearson-Aitken selection models in umxRAM}
\description{
When a sample is selected on a variable, bare correlations mislead: selecting
high scorers on education (caused by IQ and C) induces a spurious negative
IQ-C correlation (collider bias). Pearson-Aitken selection models recover the
population moments given the known population variance of the selection
variable. umx supports two patterns:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Correction} (non-degenerate): \code{selection = list(var=, popVar=)} adds a
free \code{arrows=0} selected-variance path plus a constraint equating the
implied population variance to \code{popVar} (the identifying assumption).
Use \code{\link[=umx_make_selection_data]{umx_make_selection_data()}} to simulate the IQ/C/EA demo below.
For a selected SET (personnel battery hired on several tests),
\code{selection = list(var=, popCov=)} frees the whole selected submatrix
and constrains the implied population submatrix to \code{popCov}, recovering
each predictor's population validity (Schmidt & Hunter (1998) case).
\item \strong{Assortment} (degenerate): hand-written \code{arrows=0} cross-covariances, as
in the Maes et al. (1998) spouse model (\link{maes1998}). Here the selection
set covers every variable, so no Aitken adjustment happens: the paths are
free covariances drawn without arrowheads.
}

Means stay free but are saturated nuisance under selection, not population
quantities: interpret covariances, not means.
}
